# run.plumb -- is a keel run actually GOVERNED, or only logged?
#
# *** THIS PROGRAM HALTS ON EVERY KEEL LEDGER, AND THAT IS THE RESULT. ***
# It is kept exactly as it was first written, before the data was looked at,
# because the halt is the finding. See keel/NULL.md.
#
# The intended reasoning was ordinary: a ledger full of entries proves nothing
# on its own, because a run can fail in two different ways.
#
#   the inbound leg fails  -- things happened that never reached the kernel, so
#                             the ledger is a partial record presenting itself
#                             as a complete one
#   the outbound leg fails -- everything was recorded, but the records carry no
#                             reason, so nobody can disagree with any of it
#
# E = U x D_in x D_out, a PRODUCT on purpose: a run with a huge number of
# actions and either leg at zero is worth nothing, and multiplication is the
# only arithmetic that says so without an argument.
#
# WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENED
# The inbound leg is not measurable from the ledger at all. `sealed/attempted`
# came back as exactly 1.000 on every run in a 28-run cohort -- zero variance --
# because the ledger records everything that reaches it, by construction. A
# record cannot contain its own omissions. Substituting a leg that does vary
# (`named/attempted`) did not help: it correlates with the outbound leg at
# r = 1.000, because an action that arrives named is exactly the one that gets a
# rule. The two legs are one measurement wearing two names.
#
# So Plumb halts with VIF infinite, and refuses to report a confident number
# twice and call it corroboration. That refusal is correct, and it is the same
# limit the structural check reports elsewhere: whether everything came through
# the gate cannot be established from inside the gate.

plumb "keel-run-is-governed" {

  capacity  U    from field "actions"

  # leg 1: did everything that happened actually come through the kernel?
  encode    Din  from ratio "sealed"/"attempted"

  # leg 2: can a person disagree with the decisions? A decision with no named
  # reason and no named rule is an assertion, not a governance record.
  decode    Dout from ratio "reasoned"/"actions"

  floor 0.98

  # Whatever the run says about itself is DELETED before evaluation. Not
  # weighted less -- removed. A run cannot vouch for itself.
  blind "self_report"
  blind "summary"

  # The clause that stops this program. Keeping it is the whole point: a
  # version without it would print a number, and the number would be a lie.
  independent encode decode

  require evidence 4 of 5

  handles
  receipt
}
